    "body": "## Motivation\n\nNon-streaming `/v1/messages` requests currently turn an upstream `HTTPException(503)` into `500 api_error`. In particular, a queue-full rejection loses its overload status because the Anthropic handler catches it as a generic exception.\n\nThis fixes the non-streaming follow-up explicitly listed in #43779. It is independent of that PR and can be merged separately.\n\n## Modifications\n\n- Catch `HTTPException` before the generic exception handler and preserve its status code, mapping it through `ERROR_TYPE_MAP`. A queue-full rejection now returns `503 overloaded_error`.\n- Reuse the existing message sanitization: 4xx messages retain useful details after sanitization, while all 5xx responses continue to return `Internal server error`.\n- Log the original status code and detail server-side without duplicating the generic error-response log.\n- Add regression coverage for raised exceptions, including integer and `HTTPStatus` 503, message sanitization, diagnostic logging, and unexpected exceptions. Existing returned-error tests continue to run.\n\nThe exception handler covers both internal request conversion and non-streaming execution, so HTTP exceptions raised during conversion also retain their status. Streaming behavior is unchanged.\n\n## Accuracy Tests\n\nN/A. No model outputs or inference kernels are changed.\n\n## Speed Tests and Profiling\n\nN/A. This changes HTTP error handling only.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Before the status fix, both integer 503 and `HTTPStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE` regression cases failed with `500 != 503`.\n- Before the logging fix, both 500 and 503 logging regression cases failed because the original detail was missing.\n- After both fixes, the Anthropic unit test file passed all 71 tests on Linux x86_64 with GPUs hidden, using the repository's existing CPU kernel stub.\n- After pruning redundant subtests and dropping a `CancelledError` identity test that failed on CI's Python 3.10, the file passes all 68 tests on Python 3.10.\n- Ruff checks (`F401,F821,F823,UP037`), Ruff formatting, isort, and `git diff --check` passed for the changed files.\n\nCommand run from the repository root:\n\n```bash\nPYTHONPATH=python CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=99 OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 \\\n  python -m unittest discover \\\n    -s test/registered/unit/entrypoints/anthropic \\\n    -p test_serving.py -v\n```\n\nThe final test run used commit `0ac6b32fbeba73dd4fa63ca1967da8fed6831eea`, based on main at `a70a83bcd237b7de6136574f47515a58bf9efd83`. The environment used Python 3.12.14, PyTorch 2.13.0+cu129, and Transformers 5.19.0; this was a native unit-test run, not the full CI dependency lock or a model-server end-to-end test.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Format the changed code according to the repository's style.\n- [x] Add unit tests to the existing CPU-registered test file.\n- [ ] Update documentation (not applicable).\n- [ ] Provide accuracy and speed benchmarks (not applicable).\n- [x] Follow the repository's code style guidelines.\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:hourglass_flowing_sand: [Run #38233317036](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38233317036)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #38233316744](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38233316744)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd:start -->:x: [Run #38233317017](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38233317017)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd:end -->\n<!-- pr-states:end -->\n",
